<p>One of North Dakota's largest Greek Revival houses, the home was built in 1899 by {% person "hiram-rogers-lyon" %}Hiram R. Lyon{% endperson %} and has long been regarded as one of Mandan's outstanding residences. It stands among other historic homes in west-central Mandan, within walking distance of the then-new Queen Anne–style Northern Pacific passenger depot, on land purchased from the railroad.</p>

<p>The stacked-fieldstone foundation is approximately two and a half feet thick. Builders strengthened the structure with tongue-and-groove subsiding laid diagonally on the exterior walls. All the lumber was cut by hand, and much of it had to be specially ordered. The interior had quarter-sawn white-oak woodwork, hand-painted dining-room cabinets and wainscoting, and a matching table and chairs. The table seated 20 people.</p>

<p>The house was patterned after Southern plantation homes, with massive pillars across the front. It originally had a porte-cochère—a covered carriage entrance—on the southern side, allowing passengers to enter and leave their carriages under shelter from wind and snow.</p>

<p>Lyon arrived in Mandan in 1881. During his 25 years in the city, he served as president of First National Bank and founded the Mandan Mercantile Company. He also had interests in the Mandan Roller Mill and North Dakota Milling Association. He raised sheep and arranged for thousands of animals to be placed on shares with Morton County farms.</p>

<p>Other businesses he established included the Missouri Valley Milling Company, whose premium flour was called Lyon's Best; the Mandan Electric Company; the Mandan and North Dakota independent telephone companies; and Missouri Valley Grocery.</p>

<p>{% person "george-h-bingenheimer" %}George{% endperson %} and {% person "margaret-bowers-bingenheimer" %}Margaret Bingenheimer{% endperson %} purchased the home in 1907. George was a prominent local businessman who had established a major lumber and machinery outlet and the town's first drugstore in 1881.</p>

<p>The historic photograph, used with permission of the State Historical Society of North Dakota, shows the Lyon Home and the family's horse-drawn carriage around 1906.</p>

<p>Mandan attorney John Sullivan and his wife, Nan, lived in the house with their four children from 1920 to 1944. John and Mary Kennelly purchased it in 1944, followed by Paul and Magdalena Schaff in 1951. In 1956, Hector and Margaret Hoenig remodeled the house as Hoenig Funeral Home.</p>

<p>Tom and Kathy Weigel, later owners and operators of the funeral home, updated the building during the 1980s. A 1,700-square-foot addition was constructed on its northern side in 2011. Designers sought to preserve the building's character and its relationship to the residential neighborhood, reproducing the columns, balcony rail, roof gables, and trim from an architectural report documenting the original design.</p>
